{"text": "Apple says its biggest business risks are trade and geopolitical disruption, supply-chain concentration and component shortages, competition and pricing pressure, intellectual property/licensing risk, and cybersecurity. Its filing also says trade and other international disputes can hurt consumer spending and sentiment for Apple’s products and services.

Revenue is concentrated more by product than by geography, but both are diversified. By product, iPhone is still the largest line at 2025 net sales of 209,586 [f_c046dbb3766f], followed by Services at 109,158 [f_c046dbb3766f], then Mac at 33,708 [f_c046dbb3766f], Wearables, Home and Accessories at 35,686 [f_c046dbb3766f], and iPad at 28,023 [f_c046dbb3766f]. By geography, the largest region is the Americas at 178,353 [f_ecc531cd232e], then Europe at 111,032 [f_ecc531cd232e], Greater China at 64,377 [f_ecc531cd232e], Rest of Asia Pacific at 33,696 [f_ecc531cd232e], and Japan at 28,703 [f_ecc531cd232e].

On whether the desk holds any of that as figures: yes, it holds the revenue-by-product and revenue-by-geography figures from the latest filed period [f_c046dbb3766f] [f_ecc531cd232e]. It does not hold “biggest risks” as a single numeric figure; those are quoted risk-factor statements from the filing [f_efeae8ad65e6] [f_402b460372cd] [f_92cfed57940b] [f_350df42e6bdf] [f_0fb42ce6a9c4] [f_98d265d2f8df]."}

{"text": "I can rank only three of the five technology holdings from the evidence already on the record: AAPL, MSFT and NVDA. For those three, cash conversion over the trailing twelve months ranks as AAPL first, MSFT second and NVDA third. The prior-twelve-month comparison also shows AAPL improving by -1.05% [f_d94bbb316135], while MSFT’s and NVDA’s comparable change was not yet on the record in this turn.

On the book side, AAPL’s weight was 15.2% [f_d247dd7b6ce1] versus 14.7% [f_5a8b78a2a16a] in the previous run, a rise of 0.47% [f_0f7c1ca22e80]. MSFT was 16.0% [f_7094c45b41ac] versus 16.1% [f_3823e70c1845], and NVDA was 4.06% [f_14a2d13e8f45] versus 4.18% [f_a32051dcac77]. So among the names I could verify, AAPL is the one whose weight grew most since the prior run.

I cannot yet answer the full ranking across the five technology holdings, because the record here does not include the trailing-twelve-month cash-conversion ratios for GOOGL or the weight-change figures for all five names in one complete set. The current evidence does, however, point to AAPL as the weakest converter of the names I was able to verify on the book side only if the missing cash-conversion data later confirm that ordering; at present that part remains unresolved.", "problems": [{"rule": 3, "reason": "direction_conflict", "sentence": "S3"}]}

