{"text": "The portfolio’s worst drawdown episode over the last year ran from 2026-01-07 to 2026-03-27 and had a depth of 12.0% [f_7700ab5e754d]. The episode-window portfolio return was -12.0% [f_49eac48f2172], versus SPY at -7.80% [f_a628792fd7c8], so the book underperformed the market by 4.20 percentage points over that span.

The three largest detractors were MSFT at -26.0% [f_a88b90fb24f3], LLY at -20.6% [f_f7476b29b478], and AAPL at -4.34% [f_883f01dcde44]. Of those three, JPM had a filing-context row in the last year [f_c5cc9ceb15ec], GOOGL had filings dated in 2026 [f_7d29805cf1be], and MSFT had a filing dated April 29, 2026 referenced in the desk’s evidence [f_c247d4068255]. That means at least MSFT and GOOGL clearly had filings in the broader last-year evidence set, while the desk’s filing-window evidence for the episode itself is only partial and does not confirm a filing for every detractor in the drawdown window.

What this says for the book: the drawdown was not just market beta; the holdings-specific move was larger than the benchmark gap, and the damage was concentrated in two large growth names, with a smaller drag from AAPL. If you want the episode decomposition in a tighter form, the next thing to verify would be the exact market-versus-stock attribution for the episode window, because the current evidence gives the portfolio and SPY returns but not a fully itemized factor split.", "problems": [{"rule": 1, "reason": "unsourced_figure", "sentence": "S2"}, {"rule": 4, "reason": "date_expected", "sentence": "S4"}]}

