{"text": "AWS was 17% [f_0e919a8b0c7a] of Amazon’s revenue in the latest filed annual period, after 17% [f_0e919a8b0c7a] in the prior year and 16% in the year before that [f_0e919a8b0c7a]. So the mix has edged up by 1 percentage point over three annual periods, with the latest two years flat at 17% [f_0e919a8b0c7a].

On the market side, AMZN’s last-year return relative to SPY was -8.12% [f_b471f3537204]. The fitted beta to SPY was 1.41× [f_6ce5f415cd64], and the regression R² was 27.8% [f_94fa6a99c3e4], so SPY explained 27.8% [f_94fa6a99c3e4] of AMZN’s last-year return variation versus the benchmark.

Implication for the book: AWS is a stable, roughly one-sixth revenue stream that has inched higher, but the one-year AMZN vs SPY move was mostly not explained by broad market direction. That means the book’s AMZN relative performance was driven more by stock-specific factors than by the market beta alone."}
